{"id":"COR-2238","uuid":"1627b4d2-d3c0-4c84-b65a-42da3329920d","title":"Fact review queue (proposals.json) has no recovery path when unreadable","description":"When `proposals.json` cannot be read, every surface shows `fact review queue unreadable; verify fact consistency before relying on these facts` (`retrieval_guard.go`, `facts_read_cmd.go`, brief warnings; unified-path caveat code `proposal_state_unavailable`). The message names no command because none exists — and unlike the history projection, this is not text-fixable: proposals are user data (pending reviews), not a derived projection, so no rebuild can recover them.

Design decision needed: should there be an explicit escape hatch, e.g. quarantine the unreadable file and start an empty queue behind an explicit acknowledge-loss flag (`memories review --reset-queue` or similar)? Until that exists, the degraded state is permanent and the message can only state the effect.

